4. Manuscript 01-gauge-pmed: cite implemented a0/a1 + validated analytic coverage.
   ⚠️ TWO OPEN MANUSCRIPT-SIDE DISCREPANCIES (found 2026-08-21, auditing #11/#32
   against ~/projects/research/pmed-modern/01-gauge-pmed/manuscript/gauge-pmed.qmd).
   (b) is manuscript-text-only; (a) may imply a probmed code change depending on
   how its diagnosis lands — see docs/specs/SPEC-2026-08-21-bootstrap-calibration.md.
   NOT yet raised in the pmed-modern repo (cross-repo; needs explicit go-ahead there).
   PRECONDITION VERIFIED: the two DGPs are the same family, so these are direct
   comparisons, not cross-design analogies. Manuscript (gauge-pmed.qmd:207-212)
   C~N(0,1), A~Bern(expit(-0.2+0.8C)), M=0.6A+0.4C+e, lp=0.5A+0.7M+tau*AM+0.3C;
   grid (inst/sim/hopper/run_weakid_validation.R:58-64) is identical with an added
   `s` scaling the A-paths to sweep identification strength toward OE->0. The 48k
   grid is literally the manuscript's design continued into the weak-ID regime.
   (a) COVERAGE IS REPORTED WITHOUT A LENGTH CHECK. ⚠️ PARTLY RETRACTED 2026-08-22 —
       see docs/specs/REVIEW-2026-08-22-adversarial-refutation.md. What SURVIVES:
       manuscript Table 1 (tbl-sim) reports percentile-bootstrap coverage near 1 in
       every cell at nominal 0.95 (analytic: 0.86-0.91) and reads it as success
       ("restores coverage to nominal-or-above, confirming the ratio diagnosis"),
       with no accompanying length or informativeness measure. That is the defensible
       criticism, and it is the one thing both sides of the Canty-Davison-Hinkley vs
       DiCiccio-Efron exchange agree on.
       WITHDRAWN: (i) "8/8 cells at 1.00" — two cells store mcse 5e-04, i.e. coverage
       0.9995, one miss each; it is 6/8 at 1.000. (ii) "an interval at 1.00 is
       uninformative, not calibrated" — measured interval width is 0.80-0.83x a
       calibrated normal interval in the two strong-ID cells tested, i.e. NARROWER
       than calibrated, so length is not what buys the coverage there. (iii) the
       over-dispersion speculation — reweighting a fixed phi reproduces the analytic
       variance identically, and the refit arm's SD is correct at n=3000.
       The A1 "21x wider than |truth|" figure is NOT withdrawn: it comes from
       A1-flagged near-null draws in the 48k grid, a regime the review never entered.
       Unreplicated in the tested cells, not refuted — the two regimes can differ.
       RESOLVED 2026-08-22 (PR #33, docs/specs/FINDINGS-2026-08-22-phi-decomposition.md):
       the "0.65-0.83 under-dispersion" was a skewness artifact of an UNSTABLE SE
       (RMS ratio ~1 at n=800), not a biased formula. In the manuscript's regime the
       shipped estimator's excess variance is fold-split noise; reps>1 (shipped)
       brings the point estimate to oracle efficiency; coverage stays ~0.88 only
       because the SE estimator has CV 0.5-0.8 (oracle 0.17). Near the null, even
       the oracle SE explodes — A1's regime, and the manuscript's Application-section
       argument. The percentile arm's ~1.00 follows: each resample refits with a
       fresh partition, so it carries the reps=1 fold-split noise. Open problem, now
       well-posed: an SE estimator for the reps>1 point estimator; plus robust
       partition aggregation for binary Y. SE ESTIMATOR FOUND later 2026-08-22 (IF
       variance from full-sample nuisance fits; oracle-matching CV in 3 cells) —
       see FINDINGS §Open item 1; shipping it is a code change, not yet started.
   (b) THE ZHAN (2026) MECHANISM IS ASSERTED, BUT OUR OWN GRID FOUND IT
       WIDTH-EXPLAINED. Supplement (gauge-pmed.qmd:422-424) states weak
       identification is "signalled by divergence between the Wald and percentile
       intervals [@zhan2026]" as established mechanism, and §Validation gates lists
       it as gate (ii). Item 5's 48k-rep grid found that divergence ~97% explained
       by Wald width alone (flag coef -0.051 -> -0.002 under a flexible width
       control). R/gauge-pmed.R's own in-code comment already records this
       ("Zhan (2026)'s premise did not survive for coverage") and the package docs
       were rewritten to the corrected hierarchy 2026-07-19 (95dfc0f) — the
       manuscript supplement was not. CAREFUL FRAMING: the grid tested
       coverage-beyond-width, which is not identical to weak-ID detection per se,
       so the honest claim is "the manuscript asserts a mechanism our grid found
       width-explained," NOT "Zhan is wrong."
   Bearing on the open issues: (a) is the prior question behind #32 (BCa reshapes
   endpoints; it does not fix an over-dispersed resample distribution) and (b) is
   the theory behind #11's A2 gate. Both issues carry this analysis in comments.
5. #11 threshold validation — SCRIPTED, NOT RUN (PR #28, 2026-07-16).
   ⚠️ CORRECTION of an earlier entry here: I previously wrote that the gaugeboot
   grid's median seW_bt/seW_an = 1.82 "independently corroborates"
   weak_id_ratio_threshold=3. That claim is WITHDRAWN — the transfer does not hold.
   The stored grid has NO CI widths (verified against the artifact's own columns,
   not the script): only seW_an, seW_bt, and divW = a max endpoint DISPLACEMENT.
   Wald width is recoverable (2*z*seW_an, symmetric); percentile width is NOT
   (quantile-based; seW_bt is a replicate SD, and the skew between them IS the
   phenomenon). A sweep on the se_ratio = seW_bt/seW_an stand-in looks encouraging
   (separation peaks near 3) but a direct 64-rep check found se_ratio correlates
   with the shipped weak_id_ratio at only r ~ 0.41 (Spearman 0.53), different scale
   (medians 2.39 vs 1.66), agreeing on ">= 3" ~72% of the time. So the shipped
   threshold remains UNVALIDATED and the hopper grid is still required.
   COMPLETE 2026-07-16 (hopper job 4277259, 192/192 tasks, 0 failures, 48,000 reps
   across 24 cells; collate 4277455). CSVs committed to inst/sim/results/:
   weakid_validation_cells.csv, weakid_threshold_sweep.csv, oe_snr_threshold_sweep.csv.
   RESULT (as corrected by adversarial re-analysis of the raw 48k reps -- an earlier
   version of this block said "A2 validated, A1's premise wrong", which had the
   hierarchy BACKWARDS):
     * A2 (weak_id, ratio >= 3): direction real but ~97% WIDTH-TAUTOLOGY. Flagged
       draws have worse Wald coverage in all 24/24 cells (each > 2xSE; sign-test
       p = 6e-8; pooled -0.016, within-cell -0.030..-0.073 depending on weighting).
       BUT the flag's numerator adds nothing: regressing covW_an on flag + cell FE
       gives -0.051, which collapses to -0.002 (SE 0.003) under a flexible
       log(wid_wald) control, while controlling log(wid_pct) instead makes it
       STRONGER. The flag operates purely through "the analytic interval is narrow".
       Honest claim: narrow Wald intervals under-cover in this DGP, and the ratio is
       a usable SELF-CONTAINED PROXY for narrowness (a user has no cell reference);
       it does NOT validate the bootstrap-vs-Wald comparison as a diagnostic.
       Thresholds 1.75-4 are NOT distinguished; apparent "flatness" was an artifact
       of min-n weighting (equal-cell weighting grows -0.056 -> -0.079 over t =
       1.75 -> 4). t = 3 is a convention, kept as such.
     * A1 (oe_regular, oe_snr < 2): the STRONGER diagnostic, with semantics opposite
       to its docs. Flagged draws OVER-cover (+0.10; 22/22 cells; uncannily stable
       +0.099..+0.104 across binY / s-range / n slices). Mechanism verified: fires as
       OE -> 0; flagged intervals are median 21x wider than |truth| vs 3x unflagged,
       and Wald width 2.0x-5.2x wider within cell. Unlike A2 it SURVIVES the width
       control (+0.036, SE 0.003 residual), i.e. it carries information beyond width.
       So A1 flags width-untrustworthiness (uninformative interval), not
       under-coverage; its docs said "bootstrap CI may be invalid", which reads as a
       coverage warning and needs restating.
     * Single-DGP caveat: all 24 cells share one DGP family; no basis for claiming
       either threshold transfers.
   Doc surfaces (R/gauge-pmed.R roxygen, man/, NEWS, hopper README) rewritten to this
   hierarchy 2026-07-19. Commit c4397a1's message carries the superseded framing --
   history is immutable; this block and the README are the corrected record.
   OPEN: collator still reports only the pooled sweep (understates A2 ~3x); should
   report pooled + within-cell (weighting named) + per-cell.
   Deploying it surfaced two script bugs, both now fixed (see inst/sim/hopper/README.md):
     - hopper's R_LIBS held probmed 0.2.0.9000, which predates the weak-ID fields;
       0.3.0.9000 now installed to ~/Rlib/4.4-weakid (4.4-gauge left intact so the
       coverage grid stays reproducible), and both sbatch files prepend it.
     - `module load ... 2>/dev/null` silently failed in SLURM's non-login batch shell,
       killing a first submission (job 4277033) 192/192 at exit 127 in 0s. The scripts
       now source /etc/profile.d/modules.sh and hard-fail if Rscript is off PATH. The
       same latent bug was fixed in the coverage grid's sbatch files.
   ✅ What IS established (metric-independent, from the 16k reps): Wald coverage
   ~0.88 overall, never nominal in ANY cell — so an absent weak_id flag does not
   buy nominal coverage. Now documented user-facing (PR #28).
